<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Yingqiu</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Yingqiu was the first capital of the State of Qi, according to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/records-of-the-grand-historian">Shiji</a> the seat granted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jiang-ziya">Jiang Ziya</a>, Lord Taigong, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/king-wu-of-zhou">King Wu of Zhou</a> enfeoffed him in the north of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shandong">Shandong</a> peninsula. It is traditionally identified with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/linzi">Linzi</a>, near present-day Zibo, which remained Qi's capital for most of its history, though some scholars place it elsewhere in Shandong.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/yingqiu.jpg" alt="Yingqiu" width="320" height="357" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Zunkir · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Linzi_Zhanguo.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
